<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip I, Count of Flanders</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1143">1143</a>–<a href="/years/1191">1191</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Philip of Alsace</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> was Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> from 1168 to 1191, one of the most powerful princes of his age. He promoted Flemish towns and trade, held Vermandois through his wife Elisabeth, and acted as guardian of the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a>, but lost much of Vermandois to him by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-boves">Treaty of Boves</a> in 1185. He died at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-i-count-of-flanders.jpg" alt="Philip I, Count of Flanders" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Germain Demay · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sigillum_Philippi_Comitis_Flandrie_1170.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Philip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> was born in 1143, the second son of Count Thierry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and Sibylla of Anjou. His elder brother Baldwin died in 1150, which left him as his father's successor. His name appears on documents with the title of count as early as 1155, and in 1156 he married Elisabeth, elder daughter of Count Ralph I of Vermandois. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> 1157 his father transferred power to him before leaving with Sibylla on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>. Thierry returned in 1159 and co-ruled with Philip, who focused on economic and judicial matters, until Thierry's death in 1168.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In his father's absence Floris III of Holland tried to take Zeeland and Waasland and laid heavy taxes on Flemish merchants on the river route to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. Philip's fleet defeated him in 1158, and in 1166 Philip captured him on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a> and released him in 1167 under the treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>, which recognised Flemish suzerainty over Zeeland and Waasland. When Elisabeth's brother Ralph II abdicated in 1167, the couple inherited Vermandois, which pushed Flemish authority further south than before. Philip governed with the aid of Robert d'Aire and mediated between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII of France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a>.</p><p>Philip took the cross in 1175 and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> in 1177, intending to go as a pilgrim. He refused to command the planned invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> proposed by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-iv-of-jerusalem">Baldwin IV of Jerusalem</a>, and he refused the regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. He joined the siege of Harim and, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> sent reinforcements, accepted a ransom to lift it.</p><p>William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> blamed him for the failure of the planned campaign, but historians hold that William did not write in good faith. In 1180 Philip had his ward, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a>, marry his niece Isabelle of Hainaut, and war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> followed. After Elisabeth died in 1183, Philip II seized Vermandois. A later peace treaty recognised the cession of Vermandois to the king, though Philip kept the title of its count for life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Philip took the cross again in 1190. At the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> he was stricken by the epidemic in the crusader camp and died on 1 August 1191. His wife Theresa, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-portugal">Afonso I of Portugal</a>, brought his body back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, and he was buried at Clairvaux Abbey. His sister Margaret I and her husband Baldwin VIII succeeded him. Philip's reign has been seen as the end of one kind of feudal world, as for the first time a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ruled over a count of Flanders. The economic expansion of Flanders continued, as the number of communal charters shows. Philip was patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chretien-de-troyes">Chrétien de Troyes</a>, who was writing his Perceval, the Story of the Grail.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philip I of Flanders” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Count <b>Philip of Alsace</b> died at the siege of Acre in 1191 during the Third Crusade, and Baldwin IX was a leader of the Fourth Crusade, which took Constantinople in 1204.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 12th century the county passed by marriage to <b>Philip of Alsace</b>, count of Flanders, who lost much of it to Philip II of France in the Treaty of Boves in 1185.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-vermandois">County of Vermandois</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Acre, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.93,35.08&amp;year=1174" aria-label="Acre on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Philip I, Count of Flanders"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5364 4742h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5364 4742h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=32.93,35.08&amp;year=1174">What was here in 1174? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
